  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\le ${{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{or}\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nDividing both sides of the given inequality by ${6}^{3x-2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(49-{x}^{2})^{3}\\cdot {6}^{3x-2}}{{6}^{3x-2}} & \\le \\frac{0}{{6}^{3x-2}} \\\\ (49-{x}^{2})^{3} & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(49-{x}^{2})^{3}} & \\le \\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ 49-{x}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ -{x}^{2} & \\le -49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 49 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{49} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign again of the inequality since we divided by a negative number ($-1$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -7$ or $x\\ge 7.$",
